\begin{aligned}{s}_{4} & =(\frac{1}{5}-\frac{1}{6}) \\ {s}_{5} & =(\frac{1}{5}-\cancel{\frac{1}{6}})+(\cancel{\frac{1}{6}}-\frac{1}{7}) \\ {s}_{6} & =(\frac{1}{5}-\cancel{\frac{1}{6}})+(\cancel{\frac{1}{6}}-\cancel{\frac{1}{7}})+(\cancel{\frac{1}{7}}-\frac{1}{8}) \\ {s}_{7} & =(\frac{1}{5}-\cancel{\frac{1}{6}})+(\cancel{\frac{1}{6}}-\cancel{\frac{1}{7}})+(\cancel{\frac{1}{7}}-\cancel{\frac{1}{8}})+(\cancel{\frac{1}{8}}-\frac{1}{9})\end{aligned}


Notice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is


\begin{aligned}{s}_{N} & =\frac{1}{5}-\frac{1}{N+2}.\end{aligned}


Finally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$


\begin{aligned}{\sum }_{n=4}^{\infty }\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+1)} & =\lim_{N\to \infty }{s}_{N} \\  & =\lim_{N\to \infty }(\frac{1}{5}-\frac{1}{N+2}) \\  & =(\frac{1}{5}-0) \\  & =\frac{1}{5}\,.\end{aligned}
